  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\"><em>El mundo de atr\u00e1s</em>, published by Peter Lang Verlag in 2019 and authored by Claudio Canaparo, remains early in its citation trajectory with no recorded citations in OpenAlex as of this assessment. The work is catalogued in library systems and registered in institutional repositories, indicating baseline scholarly infrastructure recognition, but measurable downstream scholarly engagement has not yet accumulated.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">This scholarly book explores themes at the intersection of art, philosophy, and the environmental humanities, as evidenced by its classification across subjects including environmental sciences, human ecology, and Latin American philosophy. The work was formally registered with a DOI through Peter Lang Verlag, ensuring persistent identification and broad discoverability through library catalogues and digital repositories.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Library presence provides the clearest institutional signal. The book holds four recorded copies in the OpenLibrary network and appears in WorldCat, the global library union catalogue, where it is indexed under environmental sciences, science-and-humanities integration, and Latin American philosophical discourse. This distribution suggests adoption in academic library collections, though specific course-adoption data are not available in this record.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">The work is registered in the OpenAIRE research infrastructure, with two instance records linking to the published version and associated metadata. This registration makes the work discoverable through European and international open-access networks, though the extent of full-text accessibility cannot be determined from available signals.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">No citation events, event mentions, or social media discourse signals are recorded at this time. The absence of these signals is typical for works in the early phase of dissemination, particularly in humanities disciplines where citation accumulation and attention often develops slowly and unevenly. The scholarly record for this title remains open to future uptake and engagement.</p>",
